# An `else` starts its own line.
#
# A block written on its header's own line *ends* that line -- so the
# `else` belonging to the `if` cannot share it. Written this way the
# line holds an `if`, its whole then-block, and the beginning of another
# block, which is exactly the run-on the one-statement-per-line rule
# exists to prevent (see PYKING.md, "A block on its header's own line").
# On two lines it reads as what it is.
#
# This program is expected to be REFUSED. Its `.ast.out` holds the error
# message, not a program's output.

x = 2

if x > 1: print(7) else: print(8)
